--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` declares no `grants:`, so a narrowing it could not enforce is refused rather than accepted and dropped — `rule:concurrency/an-upgrade-is-spawn-shaped`, `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:concurrency/an-upgrade-is-spawn-shaped` lists four options and calls them 0006's. Three of the four --
// `limits:`, `grants:` and `on:` -- are refused at the sibling site with
// `E_SPAWN_OPTION_UNSUPPORTED`, because a `grants:` narrowing that were
// silently dropped would hand the child the parent's authority. Declaring them
// on this row would be that same drop with a different spelling, so the row
// declares `entry` and `args` and nothing else, and the call below has no
// parameter to bind to.
//
// This is the fail-closed direction stated as a case: what a reader has to be
// able to tell apart is a narrowing that was applied from one that was
// accepted and ignored, and only a refusal does that.
Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs", grants: {});
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0486]: no parameter of this call can be filled by the name `grants`
   --> case.nvs:13:42
   |
13 | Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs", grants: {});
   |                                          ^^^^^^ no such parameter
   = help: the parameters are: `entry:`, `args:`

error: aborting due to 1 error
